      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 1: THE TEXT ITSELF
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="text">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Text Itself</h2>

        <p><strong>Numbers 16–17</strong> (Hebrew 16:1–17:28; English 16:1–17:13). The
        narrative sits immediately after the law of the <em>tzitzit</em>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 15:37-41">Numbers 15:37–41</a>) and immediately
        before the priestly charter of <a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 18">Numbers 18</a>
        — a placement that later interpreters mined heavily.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Coalition</h3>

        <p>Four parties are named in <a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 16:1-2">16:1–2</a>:</p>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Party</th><th>Lineage</th><th>Grievance</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Korah</strong></td><td>son of Izhar, son of Kohath, son of Levi</td><td>Levitical claim on priestly prerogative</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Dathan and Abiram</strong></td><td>sons of Eliab, tribe of Reuben</td><td>Moses' civil authority; the failed exodus</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>On son of Peleth</strong></td><td>Reuben</td><td><em>Never mentioned again</em></td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>250 chieftains</strong></td><td><em>nesi'ei 'edah</em>, "men of renown"</td><td>Lay claim to sacral access</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Reuben and Kohath camp on the same (south) side of the tabernacle in
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 2">Numbers 2</a>–3 — a narrative detail that
        explains the coalition's formation and is one of the better arguments for the
        chapter's coherence.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Two Distinct Charges</h3>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          All the congregation are holy, every one of them, and the LORD is among them;
          why then do you exalt yourselves above the assembly of the LORD?
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 16:3">Numbers 16:3</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p><strong>Korah and the 250.</strong> This is a <strong>cultic</strong> and, in
        form, a <strong>democratising</strong> argument — and it is built out of
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 19:6">Exodus 19:6</a>, "a kingdom of priests and
        a holy nation." The premise is Scripture's own. The inference is what is on trial.</p>

        <p><strong>Dathan and Abiram</strong>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 16:12-14">16:12–14</a>) refuse the summons and
        accuse Moses of bringing them <em>out of</em> a land flowing with milk and honey
        — Egypt, in a deliberate inversion — to die in the wilderness, and of making
        himself "a prince over us." This is <strong>political</strong>, contains no
        priestly interest, and never mentions Aaron.</p>

        <p>Moses' responses bifurcate correspondingly: a censer ordeal for the incense
        claimants (16:5–7, 16–18), and for the Reubenites the protest of an honest
        magistrate — <em>"I have not taken one donkey from them"</em>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 16:15">16:15</a>), the formula Samuel will
        reuse in <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Samuel 12:3">1 Samuel 12:3</a>.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Two Distinct Judgments</h3>

        <ul>
          <li>The earth opens and swallows Dathan, Abiram, and their households
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 16:31-33">16:31–33</a>).</li>
          <li>Fire from the LORD consumes the 250 who offered incense
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 16:35">16:35</a>).</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Structural Spine</h3>

        <p>The episode is built as an escalating ladder, and reading it as a unit matters:</p>

        <div class="tg-timeline">
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">16:1–14</span><span>Challenge — two coalitions, two grievances</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">16:16–35</span><span>Ordeal and double judgment</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">Sign #1</span><span>The censers hammered into altar plating, "a reminder that no outsider (<em>zar</em>), who is not of the seed of Aaron, should draw near to burn incense" (17:1–5 Heb / 16:36–40 Eng)</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">17:6 / 16:41</span><span>Renewed challenge — the whole congregation blames Moses and Aaron "for the people of the LORD"</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">17:11–13</span><span>Plague — and Aaron with a censer <strong>standing between the dead and the living</strong></span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">Sign #2</span><span>Aaron's staff that buds — a <em>non-lethal</em> vindication (17:16–26 / 17:1–11)</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">Num 18</span><span>Charter — Levites given to the priests; priests "bear the iniquity of the sanctuary" so that wrath does not come again</span></div>
        </div>

        <p>Two features of this spine constrain every later use of the passage.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Constraint One — The Narrator States His Own Purpose</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Numbers 17:5 is not a reader's inference; the text
          announces that the outcome exists to settle who may approach.</p>
          <p>Whatever else the chapter is, it is a legitimation narrative for the Aaronide
          monopoly on the altar. This is not a hostile reading — it is the narrator's
          stated purpose, and it should be conceded before anything else is argued from it.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Constraint Two — Moses Never Defends Himself</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The vindicated leader in this story is
          conspicuously the one who does not assert his own authority, and who argues God
          down from wrath.</p>
          <p>At the accusation he falls on his face
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 16:4">16:4</a>). At the threat of corporate
          destruction he intercedes: <em>"O God, the God of the spirits of all flesh,
          shall one man sin and you be angry with all the congregation?"</em>
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 16:22">16:22</a>). After the plague begins he
          sends Aaron to stop it. <strong>Any reading that turns the passage into a
          warrant for leaders defending their position has inverted the protagonist's
          behaviour.</strong></p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Textual Cruxes Worth Knowing</h3>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Four Genuine Difficulties</span>
          <strong>16:1</strong> — the Masoretic Text reads <em>wayyiqqaḥ Qoraḥ</em>, "and
          Korah took," with <strong>no object</strong>. The rabbinic solution is that he
          "took himself" to one side; the Septuagint reads as though from a different verb
          ("and he spoke"); many moderns emend or posit haplography. It is a genuine,
          unresolved crux at the very first word of the story.<br><br>
          <strong>On son of Peleth</strong> vanishes after v. 1.
          <em>b. Sanhedrin</em> 109b says his wife talked him out of it; some suggest a
          corrupted clan or place name.<br><br>
          <strong>Korah's own death is never narrated.</strong> 16:32 says the earth
          swallowed "all the men who belonged to Korah."
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 26:10">Numbers 26:10</a> says the earth
          swallowed them "together with Korah." But 26:11 immediately adds:
          <strong>"the sons of Korah did not die."</strong><br><br>
          <strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 11:6">Deuteronomy 11:6</a></strong>
          recounts the earth-swallowing and names only Dathan and Abiram. Korah is absent.
          This is among the strongest single data points in the compositional debate.
          4QNum<sup>b</sup> and the Samaritan tradition carry expansions harmonising
          across the episode.
        </div>
      </section><!-- /text -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 2: CANONICAL AFTERLIFE
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="afterlife">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Canonical Afterlife Inside Scripture</h2>

        <ul>
          <li><a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 26:9-11">Numbers 26:9–11</a> — the census
          preserves both the judgment and the survival of the Korahite line.</li>
          <li><a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 27:3">Numbers 27:3</a> — Zelophehad's
          daughters carefully note that their father "was not among the company that
          gathered against the LORD in the company of Korah." The phrase has become a
          legal category.</li>
          <li><a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 106:16-18">Psalm 106:16–18</a> — Dathan and
          Abiram swallowed, fire on "the company"; Aaron is "the holy one of the LORD."
          Korah is again unnamed.</li>
          <li><strong>The Korahite Psalms</strong> — superscriptions to
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 42">42</a>, 44–49,
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 84">84</a>–85, 87–88. The sons of Korah are
          temple singers.</li>
          <li><strong>Chronicles</strong> —
          <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Chronicles 6:22-38">1 Chronicles 6:22–38</a> (Heman
          the singer descends from Korah); 9:19; 26:1–19 (Korahite gatekeepers);
          <a class="ref" data-ref="2 Chronicles 20:19">2 Chronicles 20:19</a> (Korahites
          lead praise before Jehoshaphat's deliverance).</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Most Under-Used Fact in the Reception History</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The descendants of the archetypal rebel end up
          leading Israel's worship, and their compositions are canonical Scripture.</p>
          <p>Psalm 84's "I would rather be a doorkeeper in the house of my God" comes from
          the line of the man who wanted to be more than a doorkeeper. Part II returns to
          this at length; it is the single most striking thing in the whole afterlife of
          the chapter, and the polemical tradition has almost entirely ignored it.</p>
        </div>
      </section><!-- /afterlife -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 3: COMPOSITION
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="composition">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Division Line I — Composition</h2>

        <p>Numbers 16 is the textbook test case for source criticism of the Pentateuch,
        and the arguments deserve to be stated at their strongest on both sides.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Case for Composite Origin</h3>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>Two sets of accusers with unrelated grievances</strong>, addressed
          separately by Moses, never speaking to each other.</li>
          <li><strong>Two modes of death</strong>, in two locations, by two mechanisms.</li>
          <li><strong>Two vocabularies.</strong> The Dathan–Abiram strand uses "milk and
          honey," "is it a small thing," tent-dwelling, and Moses' anger, with no cultic
          terminology. The Korah/250 strand is saturated with <em>'edah</em>,
          <em>qahal</em>, <em>qadosh</em>, <em>qarab</em> ("draw near"), censers, and the
          glory appearing — the standard priestly lexicon.</li>
          <li><strong>Deuteronomy 11:6 knows a Dathan–Abiram story without Korah.</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Korah's status shifts.</strong> In some verses he leads laymen
          claiming priesthood; in others he leads <em>Levites</em> claiming priesthood —
          a different and internally contradictory conflict, since Levites already have
          sanctioned status.</li>
          <li>Psalm 106 likewise seems to know the strands separately.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Standard Reconstruction</h3>

        <p>Wellhausen → Gray → Noth → Milgrom, Levine, Knohl, Achenbach, Baden — with
        real variation between them:</p>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Conflict</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Non-P</strong></td><td>Dathan and Abiram vs. Moses' civil rule</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>P</strong></td><td>250 chieftains vs. Aaron — a <em>lay</em> claim on priesthood</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Later priestly supplement</strong> (H, per Knohl)</td><td>Korah recast as Levite, converting the story into a <strong>Levite-vs.-Aaronide</strong> status conflict, matching the settlement in Numbers 18</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>On this reading the final layer reflects real Second Temple friction between
        Levitical and Zadokite claims — the same conflict visible in
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Ezekiel 44:10-14">Ezekiel 44:10–14</a>, where Levites
        are demoted for past apostasy.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Case for Unity</h3>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>A rebellion coalition naturally contains constituencies with
          different grievances.</strong> That the priestly claimants and the political
          claimants say different things is what one expects of an actual coalition, not
          evidence of two documents.</li>
          <li><strong>Reuben and Kohath camp adjacently</strong> (Numbers 2–3). The
          alliance has a narrative cause internal to the book.</li>
          <li><strong>The doublets have a rhetorical function.</strong> The pattern
          challenge → sign → renewed challenge → sign is a recognised Near Eastern
          narrative escalation, not accidental duplication.</li>
          <li><strong>Deuteronomy is selective by design</strong> and abbreviates
          constantly; silence about Korah in a hortatory summary proves little,
          particularly since Deuteronomy has no interest in Aaronide-vs.-Levite
          distinctions (it uses "the levitical priests").</li>
          <li><strong>The distributed reconstructions do not agree with one another.</strong>
          Milgrom, Noth, Levine, and Baden partition the chapter differently. That
          instability is itself data.</li>
        </ol>

        <p>Wenham, Ashley, Sailhamer, Duguid, and Kitchen press these points; Milgrom,
        though a source critic, concedes the final form's coherence and the significance
        of the camping arrangement.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Assessment</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The chapter shows real signs of growth; the
          specific stratigraphy is not recoverable with confidence; the final form is
          deliberate, coherent, and is the form that has done all the theological work for
          three thousand years.</p>
          <p>The evidence for <em>some</em> compositional history here is genuinely strong
          — stronger than in most contested passages, because the doublets are structural
          rather than stylistic. The evidence for any <em>particular</em> reconstruction is
          considerably weaker, and the Korah-as-Levite layer is the most speculative
          element in every scheme. Notice, too, that nothing in the reception history
          below depends on settling the source question.</p>
        </div>
      </section><!-- /composition -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 4: ANCIENT JEWISH RECEPTION
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="jewish">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Ancient Jewish Reception</h2>

        <p><strong>Sirach 45:18–19</strong> — outsiders raged against Aaron; "Dathan and
        Abiram and their followers, and the company of Korah"; fire consumed them.
        Already a fused, single episode by the second century BC.</p>

        <p><strong>Pseudo-Philo, <em>LAB</em> 16</strong> — Korah's rebellion is provoked
        specifically by <strong>the law of the fringes</strong>. This exegesis exploits
        the placement of Numbers 16 directly after Numbers 15:37–41 and is a striking
        early example of reading by juxtaposition.</p>

        <p><strong>Josephus, <em>Antiquities</em> 4.11–66</strong> — the longest ancient
        treatment. Josephus makes Korah a <strong>demagogue</strong>: wealthy, eloquent,
        exploiting the crowd's resentment, cloaking ambition in the language of equality.
        Moses is the constitutional statesman who refuses to answer force with force and
        submits the case to God. Josephus is writing after AD 70 for a Roman readership
        about Jewish sedition; Korah becomes the type of the revolutionary who destroys
        his own nation.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Rabbinic</h3>

        <div class="tg-definition">
          <span class="tg-definition__term">A dispute for the sake of Heaven</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__original">maḥloqet le-shem shamayim</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__def"><em>m. Avot</em> 5:17, the decisive Jewish
          formulation. The disputes of Hillel and Shammai are "for the sake of Heaven" and
          endure; "the dispute of Korah and all his company" is <em>not</em> for the sake
          of Heaven and does not endure. <strong>The category being condemned is not
          disagreement but disagreement of a certain kind</strong> — a distinction the
          Christian polemical tradition largely lost.</span>
        </div>

        <ul>
          <li><strong><em>m. Sanhedrin</em> 10:3</strong> — whether the company of Korah
          has a share in the world to come. R. Akiva: no. R. Eliezer: yes, on
          <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Samuel 2:6">1 Samuel 2:6</a>, "the LORD kills and
          brings to life; he brings down to Sheol and raises up."</li>
          <li><strong><em>b. Sanhedrin</em> 109b–110a</strong> — Korah's fabulous wealth;
          On's wife; and the famous halakhic taunts: does a garment made entirely of blue
          still need a blue thread? Does a house full of Torah scrolls still need a
          mezuzah? Korah's argument is a <em>reductio</em> against ritual particularity —
          a genuinely clever theological position, which is precisely the midrash's point.</li>
          <li><strong>Zoharic and Hasidic readings</strong> — Korah "quarrelled with
          peace"; his error is often construed as <strong>premature messianism</strong>,
          seizing in this age a levitical elevation that belongs to the age to come. Some
          sources hint that Korah's claim will one day be vindicated <em>at the right
          time</em>.</li>
        </ul>
      </section><!-- /jewish -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 5: NT AND PATRISTIC
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="patristic">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">New Testament and Patristic Reception</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">New Testament</h3>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Jude 11</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Cain, Balaam, Korah — and the referent that is routinely ignored.</div>
          <p>"They perished in the rebellion (<em>antilogia</em>) of Korah"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Jude 11">Jude 11</a>). This single verse licenses
          virtually the whole Christian tradition of Korah-polemic.
          <strong>But note the referent:</strong> Jude's targets are people who have crept
          in among the love feasts, who "shepherd themselves" (v. 12), reject authority,
          and slander. They are false <em>teachers</em> — insiders with influence — not
          congregants questioning a leader. That exegetical control is routinely ignored.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">2 Timothy 2:19</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">The Korah verse, quoted by an apostle — and pointed somewhere unexpected.</div>
          <p><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 2:19">2 Timothy 2:19</a> quotes
          Numbers 16:5, "The Lord knows those who are his," and pairs it with "let
          everyone who names the name of the Lord depart from iniquity." The context is
          the "great house" containing vessels of honour and dishonour. Paul reads Korah
          as a lesson about the church as a <strong>mixed body</strong> whose true
          membership God alone certifies — a strikingly different application from the
          hierarchical one.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Hebrews 5:4</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">The anti-Korah principle, applied to Christ.</div>
          <p>"No one takes this honour for himself, but only when called by God, just as
          Aaron was" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 5:4">Hebrews 5:4</a>). This is the
          pivotal text: the New Testament's answer to Korah is not a new Aaronide order
          but <strong>Christ</strong>.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Patristic</h3>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>1 Clement 43, 51</strong> (c. AD 96) — the most consequential early
          use. Written to Corinth, where presbyters had been deposed, Clement deploys
          <strong>Aaron's budding staff</strong> (ch. 43) as proof that God certifies his
          own ministers, and Dathan and Abiram (ch. 51) against those who make sedition.
          This is the seed of the entire "apostolic order" argument.</li>
          <li><strong>Origen, <em>Hom. Num.</em> 9</strong> — allegorical: Korah is heresy;
          being swallowed by the earth is being consumed by earthly-mindedness. The
          censer-plating figures the church absorbing what heresy leaves behind.</li>
          <li><strong>Cyprian</strong> (<em>Epp.</em> 3, 43, 59, 67; <em>De unitate</em>)
          — the most aggressive user. Against Novatian and Felicissimus, Korah becomes the
          definitive type of the <strong>schismatic</strong>: one altar, one priesthood,
          and whoever erects a rival one is Korah. Cyprian's use is where Korah becomes a
          permanent weapon of ecclesiastical polemic.</li>
          <li><strong>Chrysostom, <em>On the Priesthood</em></strong> — the danger of
          <em>desiring</em> the office.</li>
          <li><strong>Ambrose, Jerome, Augustine</strong> — Augustine deploys it against
          the Donatists, but his more distinctive contribution is on the sons of Korah:
          grace is not cancelled by a father's sin, and the church contains wheat and
          tares until the harvest.</li>
          <li><strong>Gregory the Great, <em>Regula Pastoralis</em></strong> — against
          those who grasp at rule; and, notably, against those who exercise it for their
          own honour.</li>
          <li><strong>Medieval</strong> — standard artillery in the investiture
          controversy, used by Gregorian reformers against lay investiture and by every
          party against every other. Aquinas cites it in the treatise on schism
          (<em>ST</em> II-II q.39).</li>
        </ul>
      </section><!-- /patristic -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 6: THE REFORMATION QUARREL
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="reformation">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Division Line II — The Reformation Quarrel</h2>

        <p>This is where the passage's logic is most sharply contested, and the exchange
        is worth reconstructing carefully, because every modern debate is a variation on it.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">The Catholic Charge</span>
          <p>Eck, Cochlaeus, later Bossuet: Luther's argument <em>is</em> Korah's argument,
          nearly verbatim. Numbers 16:3 — "all the congregation are holy" — is
          <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 2:9">1 Peter 2:9</a> in the mouth of a rebel.
          Laymen have seized the sacerdotal office. The earth-swallowing is the answer.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">The Protestant Reply — Calvin</span>
          <p><em>Comm. on Numbers</em>; <em>Institutes</em> IV.iii. Calvin makes the move
          that has defined Protestant handling ever since: <strong>Korah's premise was
          true and his inference was false.</strong> Israel <em>was</em> a holy nation.
          Korah's sin was not affirming the people's holiness but seizing an office God
          had not conferred on him. The Reformers, Calvin argues, claim no office not
          conferred by the Word and by the church's call; the papacy, by contrast, is the
          true Korah, arrogating a priesthood Christ has not given, since Christ has ended
          the sacerdotal office by fulfilling it. Calvin also draws the general lesson that
          has outlived the controversy: <strong>a true doctrine can be weaponised to
          overthrow God's ordering.</strong></p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">The Protestant Reply — Luther</span>
          <p><em>To the Christian Nobility</em> (1520) distinguishes
          <strong>priesthood</strong> (<em>Priestertum</em>) from <strong>office</strong>
          (<em>Amt</em>). All believers are priests; not all hold the ministry, which
          requires a call from the congregation. Korah's error lies on the office side,
          not the priesthood side.</p>
        </div>

        <p><strong>The magisterial Reformers then use Korah themselves</strong> — against
        Müntzer, the Zwickau prophets, and the Peasants' War. Luther's <em>Against the
        Robbing and Murdering Hordes</em> runs in this key. Anabaptists take the charge
        from both directions at once.</p>

        <p><strong>The English Civil War</strong> is where the trope saturates:
        Presbyterians against Independents, Anglicans against Puritans, royalists against
        regicides — and then, decisively, the <strong>inversion</strong>. Sectaries and
        Levellers argue that the <em>prelates</em> are Korah, arrogating to a caste what
        belongs to the whole priesthood of saints. Milton works this seam in
        <em>The Reason of Church-Government</em>.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Point at Which the Text Stops Deciding Anything</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Once the inversion is available, the text can no
          longer settle anything on its own; it can only restate whichever ecclesiology
          the arguer already held.</p>
          <p><strong>Wesley</strong> provides the cleanest later case. His ordination of
          Coke for America is precisely a Korah question — irregular orders claimed on
          grounds of necessity and calling — and his brother Charles' objection is made in
          exactly these terms.</p>
        </div>
      </section><!-- /reformation -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 7: MODERN USES
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="modern">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Modern Uses</h2>

        <p><strong>1. Ecclesiology and orders.</strong> Still the standard text in
        Catholic, Orthodox, and Anglo-Catholic arguments about ordination and succession,
        and in Protestant arguments about internal call versus self-appointment. It
        appears in debates over lay celebration of the Eucharist, over women's ordination,
        and over church-planting outside denominational authorisation.</p>

        <p><strong>2. Authoritarian and abusive use.</strong> In shepherding/discipleship
        movements, some charismatic and independent networks, and parts of fundamentalist
        Baptist practice, Korah is fused with "touch not the Lord's anointed" — a
        <em>different</em> text (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Samuel 24">1 Samuel 24</a>,
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 105:15">Psalm 105:15</a>, about Saul and about the
        patriarchs respectively) — to produce a rhetoric in which questioning a pastor is
        rebellion against God. The vocabulary is a recognised marker in the literature on
        spiritual abuse. <a href="part-5.html">Part V</a> treats this at length.</p>

        <p><strong>3. Egalitarian, liberationist, and critical readings.</strong> Korah as
        the suppressed democratic voice; the chapter as priestly propaganda naturalising
        hierarchy. Present in feminist, postcolonial, and some Jewish humanistic readings.
        Common in Reform and Reconstructionist preaching on <em>Parashat Korach</em>,
        often paired sympathetically with <em>Avot</em> 5:17.</p>

        <p><strong>4. Political theology.</strong> Deployed on every side of revolution,
        civil disobedience, and religiously grounded resistance — in loyalist sermons
        against the American Revolution, in nineteenth-century sermons for social order,
        and inverted by dissenters and abolitionists.</p>

        <p><strong>5. Jewish preaching.</strong> <em>Parashat Korach</em> is one of the
        richest annual sites of Jewish political reflection: on demagoguery, populism,
        sincerity without humility, and the difference between argument that builds and
        argument that consumes.</p>

        <p><strong>6. Academic.</strong> The Korah material as evidence for post-exilic
        Levite/Zadokite conflict; the Korahite psalm guilds as evidence of rehabilitation
        and of the complicated politics of the Second Temple clergy.</p>
      </section><!-- /modern -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 8: WEIGHT OF EVIDENCE
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="evidence">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Weight of Evidence</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Well Supported</h3>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>The passage is, on its own account, a charter for restricted
          sanctuary access.</strong> Numbers 17:5 says so explicitly.</li>
          <li><strong>Two distinct grievances — cultic and civil — are present</strong>,
          on any compositional theory.</li>
          <li><strong>The Korahite line survived and led Israel's worship</strong>
          (Numbers 26:11; 1 Chronicles 6, 9, 26; 2 Chronicles 20; the psalm titles). Any
          use of Korah to assert irrevocable damnation of a dissenting party runs against
          the canon's own follow-through.</li>
          <li><strong>Moses' modelled response is intercession, not self-defence</strong>
          (16:4, 22; 17:11–13).</li>
          <li><strong>Jude 11 does make Korah a Christian type</strong> — of false
          <em>teachers</em> inside the assembly, per the surrounding verses.</li>
          <li><strong>Hebrews 5:4 draws the legitimate structural principle</strong>:
          sacral office is conferred, not seized.</li>
          <li><strong>The rabbinic diagnosis is exegetically sound</strong>: the fault lies
          in the <em>character</em> of the dispute, not in the existence of dispute.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Genuinely Contested</h3>

        <ul>
          <li>The <strong>compositional stratigraphy</strong>, especially the date and
          setting of the Korah-as-Levite material.</li>
          <li>Whether <strong>Deuteronomy 11:6's silence is decisive</strong> or merely
          characteristic abbreviation.</li>
          <li>Whether the <strong>sons' survival carries theological weight</strong>
          (grace across generations, Ezekiel 18) or is a bare genealogical note.</li>
          <li><strong>The status of the 250</strong> — lay claimants or a Levitical
          faction — which changes what kind of conflict the story is about.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Weak — and the Hinge of Everything</h3>

        <p><strong>The analogy itself.</strong> Nearly every polemical use of Korah since
        1 Clement assumes:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Aaronide priest : Korah :: bishop / pastor / elder : dissenter
        </blockquote>

        <p>That analogy is doing all the work, and the New Testament does not obviously
        supply it. <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 7">Hebrews 7</a>–10 argues that the
        Aaronide priesthood is <em>finished</em>, superseded by Christ's;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 2:5">1 Peter 2:5</a>, 9 and
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 1:6">Revelation 1:6</a> assign the priestly
        designation to the whole people. The New Testament's own use of Numbers 16 goes to
        <strong>Christ's appointment</strong> (Hebrews 5:4) and to <strong>God's knowledge
        of the elect within a mixed church</strong> (2 Timothy 2:19) — neither of which
        grounds a clerical caste as such.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Consequence, Stated Symmetrically</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The Korah argument is only as strong as the
          ecclesiology it presupposes.</p>
          <p>If a church holds that its ministers stand in an Aaronide-analogous conferred
          order, the passage supports its polemic against unauthorised ministry. If a
          church holds that the priesthood is Christ's and the people's, the passage
          supports rather less than is usually claimed from it — chiefly that offices are
          given, not grabbed. <strong>Neither side can use Numbers 16 to establish its
          ecclesiology, because in each case the ecclesiology is the premise that makes the
          text applicable at all.</strong> The four-century inversion war after the
          Reformation is not an accident; it is the predictable result of a text whose
          polemical force is entirely downstream of a prior commitment.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">A Category Shift That Almost Always Goes Unnoticed</span>
          Numbers 16 is about <strong>who may draw near</strong> (<em>qarab</em>) to the
          sanctuary. It is not, in its priestly strand, about obedience to leaders at all.
          The obedience question belongs to the Dathan–Abiram strand — and there, notably,
          God's answer is not "submit to Moses" but a direct vindication that Moses himself
          refused to seek.
        </div>
      </section><!-- /evidence -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 9: WHAT IT WILL AND WON'T BEAR
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="bears">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">What the Passage Will and Won't Bear</h2>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">It Will Bear</span>
          <ul>
            <li>Access to God is on God's terms, not by self-assertion.</li>
            <li>Legitimate ministry is conferred, not seized (Hebrews 5:4).</li>
            <li>A true doctrine — "all the congregation are holy" — can be made an
            instrument of destruction. Orthodoxy of premise is no guarantee of the argument
            built on it.</li>
            <li>Ambition cloaked in the language of equality is a recurring and
            recognisable pattern (Josephus and <em>Avot</em> converge here).</li>
            <li>Corporate judgment is a real biblical category — and one that Moses
            successfully contests (16:22), a datum for the Ezekiel 18 trajectory.</li>
            <li>Judgment on a father does not fall automatically on the sons
            (Numbers 26:11).</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">It Will Not Bear, Without Importing Outside Premises</span>
          <ul>
            <li>That any particular ecclesiastical structure is the successor of the
            Aaronide priesthood.</li>
            <li>That questioning a leader is rebellion against God. The text's vindicated
            leader neither claims this nor acts on it.</li>
            <li>That the sincerity of a dissenter's theological argument is itself the sin
            — the rabbis located the fault in motive and manner, not in the raising of the
            question.</li>
            <li>That God's verdict on a dispute can be read off the outcome of a power
            struggle. In the text, the verdict is delivered by unmistakable theophany
            precisely because human adjudication was refused.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-application">
          <span class="tg-application__label">Where This Leads</span>
          <p>Part I has established what the chapter is and what it cannot settle.
          <a href="part-2.html">Part II</a> follows the questions it opens out into
          systematic theology — Christology, priesthood, schism, election, corporate guilt,
          intercession, and the discernment problem — and ends where the polemical
          tradition never goes: with the psalms the rebel's own family wrote.</p>
        </div>
      </section><!-- /bears -->

      <div class="tg-reading">
        <h2 class="tg-reading__title">For Further Study</h2>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Numbers</cite>, Jacob Milgrom. JPS Torah Commentary, 1990.
          <em>The standard critical-but-sympathetic treatment; strong on the priestly
          system and on why the camping arrangement matters for the chapter's unity.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Numbers 1–20</cite>, Baruch Levine. Anchor Bible, 1993.
          <em>The fullest philological treatment of the cruxes, including the object-less
          verb at 16:1.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Book of Numbers</cite>, Timothy Ashley. NICOT, 1993; and
          <cite>Numbers</cite>, Gordon Wenham. TOTC, 1981.
          <em>The best evangelical presentations of the case for the final form's
          coherence.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Sanctuary of Silence</cite>, Israel Knohl. Fortress, 1995.
          <em>The source of the influential proposal that the Korah-as-Levite material is a
          late Holiness-school supplement.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Composition of the Pentateuch</cite>, Joel Baden. Yale, 2012.
          <em>A rigorous modern documentary reading; useful precisely because its partition
          of Numbers 16 differs from Noth's and Milgrom's.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Antiquities of the Jews</cite> 4.11–66, Josephus. c. AD 93. Public domain.
          <em>The longest ancient retelling, and the origin of the "Korah as demagogue"
          reading that runs through the whole Western tradition.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>1 Clement</cite> 43, 51. c. AD 96. Public domain.
          <em>The earliest Christian appeal to ordered succession — and it is built on
          Aaron's budding rod, not on Numbers 16's fire.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Commentaries on the Four Last Books of Moses</cite>, John Calvin. 1563.
          Public domain. <em>Ad loc. — the origin of the "true premise, false inference"
          reading that has defined Protestant handling ever since.</em>
        </p>
